## 1. SCOPE & PUBLIC-DOMAIN SYSTEMS BOUNDARY (`INVARIANT 30`)

This specification formalizes the mathematical, cybernetic, and game-theoretic isomorphism between decentralized software state machines (`OPSTACK-KERNEL-v2.2`) and autonomous aerial/counter-UAS swarms. Pursuant to **Invariant 30 (22 C.F.R. § 120.34 & 15 C.F.R. § 734.7)**, this document contains **zero physical manufacturing blueprints, circuit schematics, or export-controlled munitions firmware**. It establishes pure algorithmic and control-theoretic primitives showing how the exact equations governing **anti-DDoS rate-throttling**, **client-untrusted multiplayer netcode ("anti-cheat") physics verification** (formalized below as **BFT Kinematic State-Residual Verification**), **stigmergic class-action Harm Beacons**, and **jurisdiction-bifurcated state machines** solve five foundational failure modes in contested and civil airspace.

---

## 2. VECTOR I: KADEMLIA XOR AIRSPACE DECONFLICTION & ANTI-OVERKILL INTERCEPTOR THROTTLING (`CFAA RATE-THROTTLING` ISOMORPHISM)

### 2.1 The Mechanistic Mismatch: Target Over-Subscription ("Physical DDoS")
In both civil disaster-response (wildfire thermal mapping, hurricane search-and-rescue) and defensive counter-swarm engagements, autonomous aerial fleets face the **Target Over-Subscription Paradox**:

1. **Centralized C2 Collapse:** Legacy UAV Traffic Management (UTM) and centralized air-defense C2 rely on continuous, high-bandwidth RF backhauls to a central orchestrator. During natural disasters (cellular grid collapse) or contested electronic warfare (wideband RF jamming and anti-radiation targeting of active emitters), centralized C2 fails immediately.
2. **Uncoordinated Greedy Clustering:** When autonomous nodes fall back to local greedy heuristics without central C2, multiple nodes lock onto the highest-salience signal (e.g., 8 defensive interceptors pursuing the same lead decoy drone, or 10 search-and-rescue drones scanning the same burning structure), leaving 90% of the spatial grid or trailing saturation wave unaddressed.

### 2.2 The OPSTACK Isomorphism: Terrain-Masked Corridor Scoring, Dynamic Redundancy Quotas ($K_k^*$), & Kademlia XOR Symmetry-Breaking
In `AUDIT-2026-CFAA-LAB-LIABILITY-STIGMERGIC-REMEDIATION.md` (`CFAA-LAB-LIABILITY-v1.0`), decentralized software nodes prevent server DDoS without a central coordinator by combining **Kademlia XOR distance partitioning** with **local pheromone backoff traces**. Ported to 3D physical space—where airborne inference compute is the primary bottleneck while physical airframes are low-cost and attritable—1-to-1 assignment is replaced by **Value- and Survival-Weighted Redundancy Quotas ($K_k^*$)** paired with **Terrain-Masked Path Scoring**:

1. **Terrain-Masked Corridor Survival & Kademlia XOR Tie-Breaking:**
   Rather than ranking targets by naive straight-line Euclidean distance (which exposes nodes crossing open ground) or pure uniform hashing, each node evaluates candidate spatial vectors $V_k$ by their **Terrain-Masked Survival Probability** $p_{i \to k} \in (0, 1)$ (where longer nap-of-the-earth routes masked by tree lines, gullies, or urban canyon clutter outscore shorter exposed paths), using Kademlia XOR distance $d_{\text{XOR}}(i, k) = \text{int}(H_k) \oplus \text{int}(\text{NodeID}_i)$ (where $H_k = \text{SHA-256}(\text{GeoVoxel}_k \parallel t_{\text{epoch}})$) as a zero-compute, deterministic symmetry-breaker among equivalent corridors.

2. **Dynamic Salvo Redundancy Quotas ($K_k^*$) & Stigmergic Claim Backoff (`Invariant 6`):**
   Because any individual airframe along corridor $k$ faces disablement or miss probability $(1 - p_k)$, assigning a single node ($K = 1$) is optimal only for near-certain corridors ($p_k \ge 0.95$). For general targets with priority/value $V_k$ requiring completion confidence $P_{\text{req}}(V_k) \in [0.80, 0.99]$, the required multi-node redundancy quota $K_k^*$ is governed by:
   $$1 - (1 - p_k)^{K_k^*} \ge P_{\text{req}}(V_k) \implies K_k^* = \left\lceil \frac{\ln\left(1 - P_{\text{req}}(V_k)\right)}{\ln(1 - p_k)} \right\rceil$$
   When Node $i$ commits to servicing vector $V_k$, it emits a zero-chat, microsecond line-of-sight optical or ultrasonic state commit (`Tokens_Chat = 0`):
   $$\text{Commit}_{\text{Claim}} = \langle H_k,\; \text{NodeID}_i,\; t_0,\; \tau_{\text{TTL}} \rangle$$
   Any peer node $j$ observing $\text{Commit}_{\text{Claim}}$ where $t < t_0 + \tau_{\text{TTL}}$ increments its local occupancy counter $C(H_k)$. High-probability covered targets ($p_k = 0.95$) cap at $K_k^* = 1$, whereas high-value, high-attrition vectors ($p_k = 0.50, P_{\text{req}} = 0.95$) accept up to $K_k^* = 5$ redundant nodes before triggering **stigmergic backoff** to the next-highest-scoring target. Over-subscription beyond $K_k^*$ drops to zero with zero central C2 packets and $\mathcal{O}(1)$ onboard compute overhead.

