## 3. VECTOR II: BYZANTINE FAULT-TOLERANT (BFT) KINEMATIC STATE-RESIDUAL VERIFICATION (`INVARIANT 17` ISOMORPHISM)

### 3.1 The Mechanistic Mismatch: GNSS Spoofing & Byzantine Node Capture
Legacy drone fleets trust external GNSS/GPS coordinates and peer telemetry implicitly. In contested environments, an adversary can broadcast spoofed civilian/tactical GNSS signals or electronically compromise $f$ nodes inside a swarm, causing the compromised nodes to broadcast fabricated target locations, phantom obstacles, or collision trajectories (a Byzantine traitor attack).

### 3.2 The OPSTACK Isomorphism: Bounded-Innovation Kinematic State Verification
Originating in deterministic multiplayer netcode state-verification engines (`AUDIT-2026-KERNEL-TELEMETRY-SHIELD.md`, where an authoritative state machine rejects client-reported coordinates that violate movement physics bounds), this civilian software primitive translates directly into a decentralized **Bounded-Innovation Kinematic State Verifier** (`Invariant 17` & `Invariant 23`):

1. **Bounded-Innovation Kinematic Physics Gate (Step-Residual Filter):**
   Every peer state commit reporting position $\mathbf{x}_t$ and velocity $\mathbf{v}_t$ after interval $\Delta t$ is audited locally against physical aerodynamic envelope bounds ($v_{\max}, a_{\max}$) and passive optical-flow angular bearings:
   $$\text{Valid}_{\text{Kinematics}}(j) \iff \frac{\|\mathbf{x}_t - \mathbf{x}_{t-1}\|}{\Delta t} \le v_{\max} \;\land\; \frac{\|\mathbf{v}_t - \mathbf{v}_{t-1}\|}{\Delta t} \le a_{\max}$$
   If external GNSS coordinates step faster than $a_{\max}$ relative to onboard inertial/optical-flow integration, the node immediately flags external GNSS as spoofed and shifts to dead-reckoning peer consensus.

2. **Byzantine Fault-Tolerant Quorum Partitioning ($N \ge 3f + 1$) & Cross-Observer Bearing Triangulation:**
   To defeat subtle Byzantine drift where a compromised node reports kinematically smooth but falsified coordinates ($\|\dot{\mathbf{v}}_j\| \le a_{\max}$), each peer $i$ cross-checks Node $j$'s self-reported position $\mathbf{x}_j$ against the physical line-of-sight optical/acoustic bearing unit vector $\hat{\mathbf{b}}_{i \to j}$ measured directly by Peer $i$'s passive sensors. Across a local neighborhood of $N \ge 3f + 1$ peers, a state transition is committed if and only if at least $2f + 1$ kinematically valid peers attest to consistent optical/acoustic bearing residuals ($\|\hat{\mathbf{b}}_{i \to j}^{\text{Observed}} - (\mathbf{x}_j - \mathbf{x}_i)/\|\mathbf{x}_j - \mathbf{x}_i\|\| \le \epsilon_{\theta}$). Nodes broadcasting trajectories that fail the bounded-innovation kinematic gate or diverge from the $2f + 1$ cross-observer bearing quorum are mathematically partitioned (`Invariant 23`: $\text{Quorum}_{\text{Consensus}}(\text{Node}_{\text{NonCompliant}}) = \emptyset$).

